! A hydrogen chain with a spin spiral along it -- the system P19 is validated on.
!
! One atom per cell, 5 bohr apart along z and 12 bohr from its images in x and y,
! so it is a genuinely one-dimensional magnetic chain with a real exchange
! coupling and a cheap unit cell. The spiral wavevector is in lattice
! coordinates (Elk's vqlss), so spiral_q(3) = 1/2 is the antiferromagnet and
! 1/4 turns the moment by 90 degrees from cell to cell.
!
! Two choices are deliberate and the tests depend on both:
!
!   * degauss = 0.1 rather than the 0.02 a production run would use. This is a
!     metal with a soft magnetic energy surface, and at a small smearing the SCF
!     lands in whichever nearby minimum its starting density is closest to --
!     the spiral and the supercell it is compared against then converge to
!     *different states* and disagree in the fourth decimal for a reason that
!     has nothing to do with the spiral machinery. At 0.1 the state is unique
!     and the two agree to 1e-10.
!   * the 1x1x4 k-grid is even, so it is invariant under a shift by half a
!     reciprocal lattice vector -- which is what makes E(q + G) = E(q) hold. See
!     pypresso/system/spiral.py.
 &control
    calculation = 'scf'
 /
 &system
    ibrav = 6, celldm(1) = 12.0, celldm(3) = 0.41666666666667,
    nat = 1, ntyp = 1,
    ecutwfc = 25.0,
    occupations = 'smearing', smearing = 'gaussian', degauss = 0.10
    noncolin = .true.
    nosym = .true.
    starting_magnetization(1) = 0.6
    angle1(1) = 90.0
    spiral_q(1) = 0.0, spiral_q(2) = 0.0, spiral_q(3) = 0.25
    nbnd = 6
 /
 &electrons
    mixing_beta = 0.3
    conv_thr = 1.0d-11
 /
ATOMIC_SPECIES
 H  1.008  H.pz-vbc.UPF
ATOMIC_POSITIONS (crystal)
 H 0.0 0.0 0.0
K_POINTS {automatic}
 1 1 4 0 0 0
